Link the static libraries in dependency order

The executable names its static libraries as CrossCut, MappingCore,
WindowsLib, ChkdraftLib -- each dependency ahead of the libraries that use it.
MSVC's linker searches every library for every unresolved symbol, whatever the
order, so that works there. GNU ld scans each archive once, left to right, and
only takes members that resolve symbols already outstanding: by the time
ChkdraftLib brings in its references, WindowsLib and MappingCore have been
passed over, and the link fails with

    undefined reference to `WinLib::WindowsItem::getHandle() const'

and a few hundred more like it. Name ChkdraftLib first, then WindowsLib and
MappingCore, then CrossCut, which both of those use. MappingCore also calls a
handful of functions the application defines (getPreSavePath(), the open/save
filter lists); the ChkdraftLib members providing them are already in the link
by then, so no link group is needed.

Upstream: not submitted yet
Applies-to: all environments

